============================================================
PURPOSE OF THE NEW PROMPT
============================================================

The prompt will receive TWO ShopGraph exports:

    1. shopgraph_category_manager.txt
    2. shopgraph_purchase_history.txt

Its job is to examine Category Manager and identify every Sub-Category whose:

    Category = NA

It must intelligently assign the best broad Category to each of those
Sub-Categories.

The prompt must use BOTH files as evidence.


============================================================
SHOPGRAPH TAXONOMY RULE
============================================================

The hierarchy is:

    Common Name
        ↓
    Sub-Category
        ↓
    Category

The prompt is ONLY completing:

    Sub-Category → Category

relationships.

It must NOT:

    rename Common Names
    rename Sub-Categories
    modify Purchase History
    create Common Names
    reclassify products into different Sub-Categories
    change existing non-NA Category assignments
    create another taxonomy level

The task is specifically:

    Find Category Manager rows where Category = NA
        ↓
    determine the appropriate broad Category
        ↓
    return replacement Category values


============================================================
EXISTING CATEGORY ASSIGNMENTS ARE AUTHORITATIVE
============================================================

Existing non-NA Category assignments in Category Manager are the primary
taxonomy reference.

The model should first study all existing:

    Sub-Category → Category

relationships.

These teach ShopGraph's existing broad Category taxonomy.

For example, if Category Manager already demonstrates relationships such as:

    Fresh Fruit → Food
    Fresh Vegetables → Food
    Bread → Food

then another food-oriented Sub-Category should normally reuse:

    Food

rather than creating a synonymous broad Category.

The exact categories must come from the actual supplied data.

Do NOT hard-code the examples above into the result.


============================================================
USE PURCHASE HISTORY AS SUPPORTING EVIDENCE
============================================================

shopgraph_purchase_history.txt provides additional context for understanding
what each Sub-Category actually contains.

Use relevant fields such as:

    Store
    Six-Digit SKU
    Product
    Common Name
    Sub-Category

to understand the meaning of an unmapped Sub-Category.

For example:

    Category Manager:
        Category = NA
        Sub-Category = some unfamiliar classification

The model should inspect Purchase History rows belonging to that Sub-Category
and study their Common Names and Product descriptions.

This allows the model to determine what the Sub-Category represents before
assigning its broad Category.


============================================================
CLASSIFICATION PRIORITY
============================================================

For every Category Manager row where:

    Category = NA

use this reasoning order:

1. Study all existing non-NA Sub-Category → Category relationships.

2. Determine whether the unmapped Sub-Category clearly belongs to one of the
   established broad Categories.

3. Inspect Common Names / Products already associated with that Sub-Category
   in Category Manager.

4. Inspect corresponding Purchase History records for additional evidence.

5. Prefer an EXISTING broad Category whenever it genuinely represents the
   Sub-Category.

6. Avoid creating near-duplicate broad Categories that differ only in wording,
   capitalization, singular/plural, or minor phrasing.

7. Do not force a genuinely different domain into an inappropriate existing
   Category merely to avoid creating a new Category.

8. Create a new broad Category only when the Sub-Category represents a
   materially different domain not adequately represented by the existing
   taxonomy.


============================================================
NEW BROAD CATEGORIES
============================================================

ShopGraph may eventually contain purchases across many domains.

Examples might include:

    food
    household goods
    automotive
    tools
    electronics
    office supplies
    garden
    furniture
    pet supplies
    clothing
    services

Do not assume every Sub-Category belongs to the existing grocery-oriented
taxonomy.

If an unmapped Sub-Category clearly represents a genuinely new domain, the
model may create a new broad Category.

A new Category should:

    be concise
    be human-readable
    represent a broad purchasing domain
    be broader than Sub-Category
    support future comparable Sub-Categories
    match the naming style of existing Categories
    not describe a retailer
    not simply repeat the Sub-Category name unless that is genuinely the
        correct broader concept

Do not create new Categories unnecessarily.


============================================================
UNCERTAINTY
============================================================

Make the best evidence-based classification when reasonable.

Use:

    NA

only when the available Category Manager and Purchase History evidence is
genuinely insufficient to determine a reasonable broad Category.

Do not fabricate unsupported classifications merely to avoid NA.

However, do not use NA merely because classification requires ordinary
semantic inference.


